---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Tldr, Eli5, WhyItMatters, HowItWorks, FailureModes, WhatILearned, DeepDive } from '../components/blocks';
import Sources from '../components/Sources.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Phishing"
  description="Phishing doesn't hack your computer. It hacks your trust. Here's how to read a URL like an attacker built it."
>
  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/security/">← Security</a></p>
  <h1>Phishing</h1>

  <Tldr>
    Phishing skips your firewall, antivirus, and every technical defense
    entirely — because it doesn't attack your computer, it attacks your
    <strong>trust</strong>. An attacker impersonates someone you already
    believe: your bank, your boss, a delivery service. They don't need to
    break in if you'll open the door yourself. The whole attack usually
    rides on one thing: a link or address that looks real enough not to
    double-check.
  </Tldr>

  <Eli5>
    A burglar could spend an hour picking your lock. Or they could just
    put on a uniform, knock, and say "delivery for you" — and you open
    the door yourself, no lock-picking required. Phishing is the second
    option, done over email or text instead of your front door:
    <ul>
      <li>the uniform is a fake sender name ("PayPal Support")</li>
      <li>the delivery box is a link that looks like your bank's website</li>
      <li>opening the door is typing your password into a page that isn't actually your bank</li>
    </ul>
    Nothing gets "hacked" in the movie-hacker sense. You get asked nicely,
    by someone pretending to be trustworthy — and most of the defense is
    just learning to check who's actually knocking before you open the door.
  </Eli5>

  <WhyItMatters>
    <ul>
      <li>Phishing is the <strong>most common entry point</strong> into a company network — not a fancy zero-day.</li>
      <li>One click on one employee's laptop can be the whole breach.</li>
      <li>You already have the exact skill needed to stop it: <strong>reading a URL carefully</strong>. Most people just never learned how.</li>
    </ul>
  </WhyItMatters>

  <HowItWorks>
    <p>Every phishing link leans on the same trick: make the fake look real at a glance.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Spoof the sender</strong> — display name says "PayPal," the real address doesn't.</li>
      <li><strong>Spoof the domain</strong> — bury the real host in a long, official-looking string.</li>
      <li><strong>Add urgency</strong> — "verify now or your account is suspended in 24 hours."</li>
      <li><strong>Harvest the click</strong> — a lookalike login page that just emails your password to the attacker.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Step 2 is the one you can actually check yourself. That's the lab below.</p>
  </HowItWorks>

  <section class="lab" aria-labelledby="lab-heading">
    <h2 id="lab-heading" class="eyebrow section-heading">Try it yourself: URL inspector</h2>
    <p class="lab-note">
      Paste any URL. Runs entirely in your browser — nothing is sent
      anywhere. These example buttons are fake, illustrative addresses
      only, not real links.
    </p>

    <div class="examples">
      <button type="button" class="ex-btn" data-url="https://www.paypal.com/signin">Clean example</button>
      <button type="button" class="ex-btn" data-url="http://paypal.com-secure-login.verify-account-update.tk/signin">Lookalike domain</button>
      <button type="button" class="ex-btn" data-url="http://paypal.com@203.0.113.5/login">Userinfo trick</button>
    </div>

    <label for="url-input" class="pw-label">URL to inspect:</label>
    <input id="url-input" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="paste a URL" />

    <div class="domain-callout" id="domain-callout" aria-live="polite">
      <span class="dc-key">The site that actually owns this URL:</span>
      <span class="dc-val" id="dc-domain">—</span>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="check-table" id="check-table" aria-live="polite">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Check</th><th>Result</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="check-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FailureModes>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"It has HTTPS, so it's safe."</strong> HTTPS only means the connection is encrypted — attackers get free certificates too. It says nothing about who's on the other end.</li>
      <li><strong>Reading left to right.</strong> The real domain is the part right before the first single <code>/</code>, counting from the end — not whatever appears first in the URL.</li>
      <li><strong>Trusting the display name.</strong> "PayPal Support" is just text anyone can type as their sender name. Check the actual address.</li>
    </ul>
  </FailureModes>

  <DeepDive>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>SPF/DKIM/DMARC.</strong> Email authentication standards
        that let a receiving mail server check whether a message claiming
        to be from yourbank.com actually came from yourbank.com's own
        servers. They genuinely help — a well-configured DMARC policy
        blocks a lot of spoofed-sender phishing. What they <em>don't</em>
        do is verify the domain isn't a lookalike: secure-yourbank-login.tk
        passes SPF/DKIM/DMARC perfectly fine, because it really is sending
        from secure-yourbank-login.tk — that domain just isn't your bank.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>IDN homograph attacks.</strong> Internationalized domain
        names allow non-Latin characters that render identically to Latin
        ones (a Cyrillic "а" next to a Latin "a," for example). A URL can
        look pixel-perfect while being encoded as something completely
        different underneath — exactly what the punycode check in the lab
        above is watching for.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Why "look for the padlock" stopped working.</strong> Free,
        automated certificate authorities made HTTPS free and instant for
        anyone, attackers included. The padlock used to correlate with "a
        real company paid for verification"; today it only means "this
        connection is encrypted" — which costs an attacker nothing either.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DeepDive>

  <WhatILearned>
    <ul>
      <li>Before clicking, find the real domain: the part just before the first single <code>/</code>, reading from the end.</li>
      <li>Urgency is a red flag on its own — real companies rarely demand action in the next 24 hours.</li>
      <li>When unsure, don't click the link at all. Open the site yourself, from a bookmark or a search.</li>
    </ul>
  </WhatILearned>

  <Sources
    sources={[
      {
        title: 'Anti-Phishing Working Group (APWG)',
        url: 'https://apwg.org/',
        note: 'The industry group that tracks and publishes real phishing trend data.',
      },
      {
        title: 'dmarc.org',
        url: 'https://dmarc.org/',
        note: 'How SPF, DKIM, and DMARC actually work — the email-authentication standards mentioned above.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Google Safe Browsing — check a URL',
        url: 'https://transparencyreport.google.com/safe-browsing/search',
        note: "Check whether a specific link is already flagged as phishing or malware, before you click it.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import { scrambleText as decode } from '../lib/decode';
  import { registrableDomain, runChecks } from '../lib/phishing';

  const input = document.getElementById('url-input') as HTMLInputElement;
  const domainEl = document.getElementById('dc-domain')!;
  const body = document.getElementById('check-body')!;
  const buttons = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.ex-btn');

  // Decode effect: results resolve through noise glyphs before settling,
  // same visual language as the AI lab and password-cracking page. Core
  // animation lives in src/lib/decode.ts; this page just owns the
  // 'decoding' glow class around it.
  function scrambleText(el: HTMLElement, finalText: string) {
    if (el.textContent !== finalText) el.classList.add('decoding');
    decode(el, finalText, { onDone: () => el.classList.remove('decoding') });
  }

  function update() {
    const raw = input.value.trim();
    if (!raw) {
      domainEl.textContent = '—';
      body.innerHTML = '';
      return;
    }
    const checks = runChecks(raw);
    if (!checks) {
      domainEl.textContent = 'not a valid URL';
      body.innerHTML = '';
      return;
    }
    let url: URL;
    try { url = new URL(raw); } catch { return; }
    scrambleText(domainEl, registrableDomain(url.hostname));

    body.innerHTML = '';
    for (const c of checks) {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      tr.className = c.flag ? 'row-alert' : '';
      const labelTd = document.createElement('td');
      labelTd.textContent = c.label;
      const resultTd = document.createElement('td');
      resultTd.className = c.flag ? 'ct-flag' : 'ct-ok';
      const detailTd = document.createElement('td');
      detailTd.textContent = c.detail;
      detailTd.className = 'ct-detail';
      tr.append(labelTd, resultTd, detailTd);
      body.append(tr);
      scrambleText(resultTd, c.flag ? 'FLAG' : 'ok');
    }
  }

  input.addEventListener('input', update);
  buttons.forEach((btn) => {
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      input.value = btn.dataset.url ?? '';
      update();
    });
  });
</script>

<style>
  .lab { margin: var(--sp-6) 0; padding: var(--sp-4); border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--bg-1); }
  .section-heading { margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
  .lab-note { color: var(--text-2); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-bottom: var(--sp-4); }

  .examples { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sp-2); margin-bottom: var(--sp-4); }
  .ex-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3);
    background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright); color: var(--text-1); cursor: pointer;
  }
  .ex-btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--text-0); }

  .pw-label { display: block; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--text-1); margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
  #url-input {
    width: 100%; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step-0); padding: var(--sp-3);
    background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright); color: var(--text-0);
  }
  #url-input:focus { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }

  .domain-callout {
    margin-top: var(--sp-4); padding: var(--sp-3); background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright);
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sp-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  .dc-key { color: var(--text-1); }
  .dc-val { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; word-break: break-all; transition: text-shadow 150ms; }
  .dc-val.decoding { text-shadow: 0 0 6px var(--accent), 0 0 14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 60%, transparent); }

  .table-scroll { margin-top: var(--sp-5); overflow-x: auto; }
  .check-table { width: 100%; min-width: 34rem; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); }
  .check-table th { text-align: left; color: var(--text-2); font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-2) var(--sp-1); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-bright); }
  .check-table td { padding: var(--sp-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--text-1); vertical-align: top; }
  .check-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
  .ct-flag { color: var(--status-broken); font-weight: 700; }
  .ct-ok { color: var(--status-working); font-weight: 700; }
  .ct-detail { color: var(--text-2); }
  .ct-flag.decoding, .ct-ok.decoding { text-shadow: 0 0 6px currentColor, 0 0 14px currentColor; }
  .row-alert { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--status-broken) 8%, transparent); }
  .row-alert td { border-bottom-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--status-broken) 30%, transparent); }
</style>
